---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;

// Grouped by where the work happens, which is also the order these became widely
// available. The last three share a group because they share a tool: what separates
// them is how much you hand over, not what you installed.
const editorAndChat = [
  {
    where: 'In your editor',
    since: 'since 2021',
    modes: [
      {
        n: 1,
        name: 'Tab-complete',
        what: 'Your editor finishes the line you were already writing.',
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    where: 'In a chat window',
    since: 'since 2022',
    modes: [
      {
        n: 2,
        name: 'Chat assistant',
        what: 'You describe a problem, then copy/paste the answer into your code yourself.',
      },
    ],
  },
];

const assistantGroup = {
  where: 'In a coding assistant',
  since: 'since 2023',
  note: 'These three are the same tool, driven differently.',
  modes: [
    {
      n: 3,
      name: 'Tool use',
      what: 'The assistant calls a tool instead of guessing — reading a file, querying a dataset, running a command. Plugins, MCP servers, and skills are all ways of handing it a tool.',
    },
    {
      n: 4,
      name: 'Planning mode',
      what: 'The assistant proposes what it intends to do, and waits for you before it does it.',
    },
    {
      n: 5,
      name: 'Autonomous agent teams',
      what: 'Long-running agents work broadly on their own and coordinate sub-agents, with few checkpoints before they act.',
    },
  ],
};

const failureModes = [
  'Unit confusion',
  'Precision loss',
  'Subtle physics violations',
  'Visualization misinterpretation',
];

const traps = [
  {
    name: 'Context bloat',
    line: 'More context is not more signal.',
    body: 'Long sessions drift as the window fills. Details you established early get buried, and nothing warns you when they do.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Over-trust',
    line: 'Fluent is not correct.',
    body: 'Confident output that is quietly wrong is the failure you will meet most often, and nothing in the tone of an answer tells you whether you are looking at one.',
  },
];

const deliverables = [
  {
    name: 'The Framework',
    when: 'July 2026',
    body: 'Tool-agnostic guidance for structuring agents, skills, and MCP servers in scientific workflows, organized around validation loops, human oversight, and domain-specific safeguards.',
  },
  {
    name: 'The Community Repository',
    when: 'October 2026',
    body: 'A starter set of scientific MCP server templates, skills such as unit checkers and conservation-law validators, and agent configuration examples.',
  },
  {
    name: 'The Reference Workflow',
    when: 'October 2026',
    body: 'A structured exercise: build a small weather-model error-statistics application with the AI tooling of your choice, and practice catching documented failure modes while you do it.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Tutorials and workshops',
    when: 'January and April 2027',
    body: '“Building Your First Scientific Agent,” followed by live sessions with real-time Q&A, recorded and published for anyone who could not attend.',
  },
];

const notScope = [
  'It produces guidance and educational resources. It does not produce production-grade AI tools, and it does not maintain long-term software infrastructure.',
  '“Comprehensive methodology” means structured and principled. It does not mean exhaustive coverage of every scientific domain or every AI tool.',
  'The Community Repository is a curated starter set that demonstrates good practice. It is not a package registry or a software distribution platform.',
  'The project does not cover new numerical weather prediction models, commercially deployable agent systems, or maintenance of contributed tools beyond April 2027.',
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Overview — Scientific Agentic Engineering"
  description="What Scientific Agentic Engineering is for: the failure modes AI agents introduce in scientific software, the ways people work with these tools, and what this fellowship is and is not building."
>
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900">Overview</h1>

    <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
      AI coding agents got good quickly, and research software teams are already using them. The
      productivity is real. What has not kept pace is the practice of checking their output, and
      scientific software fails in ways that general-purpose tooling is not looking for.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-12 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900">Why scientific code is different</h2>

    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
      Plenty of software is high-stakes. A payments system that drops a transaction and a flight
      controller that mishandles an altitude can both do real harm. But in each case there is a right
      answer, and something in the system is built to notice when it fails to appear: ledgers get
      reconciled, alarms fire, a user says the number is wrong.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
      What sets scientific software apart is not how much the answer matters. It is how hard it is to
      tell that the answer is wrong. The output is a quantity nobody knew in advance, which is the
      reason for computing it at all.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
      Some of it can still be checked. Analytic solutions, benchmark cases, and observations all give
      you something to compare against, and where they exist you should be using them. They do not
      cover most of what a working scientific code does on a given day. For the rest, a test can only
      assert what you already knew to expect, so an error that still yields a physically plausible
      number passes every check you have. The result is <strong class="text-gray-900">corrupted results that still pass CI</strong>: the program runs, the suite goes green, and the figure goes into the paper.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-4 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
      The errors that behave this way are domain errors, and a linter has nothing to say about them:
    </p>

    <ul class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
      {failureModes.map(m => (
        <li class="rounded-full bg-gray-100 px-3 py-1 text-sm text-gray-700">{m}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <p class="mt-4 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
      Plausible output is what a language model is built to produce. In most domains that is the whole
      point of it. Here it is also the hazard.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
      Reproducibility erodes the same way, and more quietly. A result nobody can reconstruct, because
      nobody recorded which parts an agent wrote or who approved them, is a result that cannot be
      defended.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-12 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900">Two reasonable reactions</h2>

    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
      Research software has not settled on these tools. Some teams adopted them quickly, and are
      finding that review practices built to catch human mistakes do not catch machine ones. Others
      have stayed away, on the reasonable grounds that a plausible wrong answer is worse than no
      answer at all.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
      Both reactions are responding to something true. This project is about what has to be in place
      before a team can take the speed without accepting the failure modes as the price of it.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-12 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900">Five ways people work with AI on code</h2>

    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
      These became widely available in roughly the order below: first in editors, then in chat
      windows, then in coding assistants. That order is why people describe them as stages. They are
      not stages. Nothing further down the list is better, each arrived without retiring the one
      before it, and most people move between them by task. Laying groundwork with an agent and then
      going back over the details by hand is an ordinary afternoon, not a regression.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
      {editorAndChat.map(({ where, since, modes }) => (
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-gray-50 p-5">
          <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3">
            <h3 class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-500">{where}</h3>
            <span class="text-xs text-gray-400 tabular-nums">{since}</span>
          </div>
          <ul class="mt-4 space-y-4">
            {modes.map(({ n, name, what }) => (
              <li class="flex gap-3">
                <span class="mt-0.5 flex h-6 w-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full border border-gray-300 bg-white text-xs font-semibold tabular-nums text-gray-600">{n}</span>
                <div>
                  <h4 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">{name}</h4>
                  <p class="mt-1 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">{what}</p>
                </div>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="mt-4 rounded-lg border border-blue-200 bg-blue-50/60 p-5 sm:p-6">
      <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3">
        <h3 class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-blue-700">{assistantGroup.where}</h3>
        <span class="text-xs text-blue-500 tabular-nums">{assistantGroup.since}</span>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-blue-900/80 leading-relaxed">{assistantGroup.note}</p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-4">
        {assistantGroup.modes.map(({ n, name, what }) => (
          <li class="flex gap-3">
            <span class="mt-0.5 flex h-6 w-6 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full border border-blue-300 bg-white text-xs font-semibold tabular-nums text-blue-700">{n}</span>
            <div>
              <h4 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">{name}</h4>
              <p class="mt-1 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">{what}</p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-6 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
      That last group is the one worth dwelling on. A coding assistant such as Claude Code or Codex
      can be driven at any of the three, so <strong class="text-gray-900">agency is a matter of degree, not a category</strong>. What separates them is how much you hand over, not which tool you installed.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
      What changes as you hand over more is how much of the work proceeds without an active decision
      from you. Watching a session scroll past is not the same as deciding anything, and once
      sub-agents are running in parallel, keeping up with all of them becomes considerably more
      taxing. Attention is the real constraint. Where to spend it, and what to check where you cannot,
      is what the <a href={`${base}framework/`} class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">framework</a> is for.
    </p>

    <h3 class="mt-10 font-semibold text-gray-900">Two risks common to all five</h3>

    <div class="mt-4 grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5">
      {traps.map(({ name, line, body }) => (
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 p-5">
          <h4 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">{name}</h4>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm font-medium text-blue-600">{line}</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">{body}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <h2 class="mt-12 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900">What this project is building</h2>

    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
      The fellowship runs from April 2026 through April 2027 and produces four things.
    </p>

    <dl class="mt-5 space-y-5">
      {deliverables.map(({ name, when, body }) => (
        <div>
          <dt class="flex items-baseline gap-3 flex-wrap">
            <span class="font-semibold text-gray-900">{name}</span>
            <span class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-gray-400">{when}</span>
          </dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">{body}</dd>
        </div>
      ))}
    </dl>

    <p class="mt-6 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
      The aim throughout is to turn broad AI capability into reliable development instruments: tools
      you can use on real scientific work and still defend the result afterward.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-12 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900">What this project is not</h2>

    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
      This is a project about guidance and education rather than a software product. Specifically:
    </p>

    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3">
      {notScope.map(s => (
        <li class="flex gap-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-gray-300" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span>{s}</span>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <h2 class="mt-12 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900">Why the framework is tool-agnostic</h2>

    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
      This project’s subject matter changes underneath it. MCP specifications take breaking changes
      and agent frameworks get rewritten. A tool named in a document written today may be deprecated
      or superseded before the fellowship ends.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
      So the framework commits to principles rather than products. Validation loops, human oversight,
      and domain-specific safeguards are claims about what has to be true of a workflow, not about
      which vendor you buy from, and they should still hold once the current generation of tools is
      gone. Where specific tools appear, they appear as examples.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-12 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900">Where to go next</h2>

    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
      The <a href={`${base}framework/`} class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">framework</a> develops the principles in depth and says where the checks go. The <a href={`${base}glossary/`} class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">glossary</a> defines the vocabulary used across the project, including every term on this page.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-8 rounded-lg bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 px-5 py-4">
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
        <strong class="text-gray-900">A note on institutional policy.</strong> Always follow your institution’s current guidance on AI-tool use, especially in sensitive roles. Tools and policies are both evolving quickly, and this site is not a substitute for either.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
